# The single VPS of production on the load bench (plano-escala LOAD-01).
#
# Production runs on one VPS with 6 shared vCPU (section 3 of the plan),
# where Traefik, the API's processes, Postgres and Redis share the same
# cores. A bench with more cores than that measures a machine nobody runs.
# This file pins the whole stack to cores 0-5, all of them sharing like on
# the VPS, and k6 to 6-9, outside them, so the generator never takes the
# CPU it measures. Use it beside the harness' own env file:
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env --env-file tests/load/vps-6vcpu.env \
#     -f tests/load/compose.load.yaml up -d --build
#   docker compose --env-file .env --env-file tests/load/vps-6vcpu.env \
#     -f tests/load/compose.load.yaml --profile k6 run --rm k6 run /load/smoke.js
#
# It needs a Linux machine with at least 10 cores (section 6 of the plan,
# the indicative bench; never the VPS itself): Docker refuses a cpuset the
# machine does not have ("Requested CPUs are not available"). The cpuset
# imitates cores, not the VPS's steal: the numbers are indicative.
#
# Only the CPU here. The memory of each phase (section 3: phase A, api
# 2 × 1024 MiB with a 576 heap; phase C, api 2 × 768 with 448 and the
# worker 1 × 1024 with 512 and 1.5 CPU) goes in API_MEMORY_LIMIT,
# API_HEAP_MB, LOAD_WORKER_MEMORY_LIMIT, LOAD_WORKER_HEAP_MB and
# LOAD_WORKER_CPUS, set for the run being measured.
LOAD_TRAEFIK_CPUSET=0-5
LOAD_API_CPUSET=0-5
LOAD_WORKER_CPUSET=0-5
LOAD_REALTIME_CPUSET=0-5
LOAD_INGEST_CPUSET=0-5
LOAD_PG_CPUSET=0-5
LOAD_REDIS_CPUSET=0-5
LOAD_K6_CPUSET=6-9
